Z||000006||GarethWright||07-30-2001||04:41 PM||||The original minis were great for racing, small and fast, the major thing was that they were FWD(which was rare in mid 50s) so you could throw it into corners.  The RWDs would spin out trying to corner at the same speed.  The new BMW Minis are a foot shorter than the Chevy Metro Coupe (that's still larger than the original), and the Mini Cooper S will have a supercharged 4 cyl putting out 160hp, that will be out in '02.<BR>Also, keep in mind the Beetle was designed in the 30's and the Mini the 50's, so it will be 2030 or so before people will be nostalgic for the 80's designs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 07-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||AusFiero||08-02-2001||05:43 AM||||And here it is.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mini.jpg"> <BR>Actually the supercharged Cooper S will have  163 HP Gareth. So I suppose 160 was close enough.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Got an article on it in front of me. It was enough power to propel the Cooper S around BMWs test track 25 seconds faster than the non supercharged version. A LHD American release one used for the test. There is rumours also apparently that there is an M version due out after the release with a 2.0 ltr turbo diesel engine putting out  241 HP. Would love to see how many a seconds a lap thats going to take off a standard one. <P>They get re-released here next year too. Seems we will get them cheaper than you guys have to pay though. Australian release price from another article shows them at about $17,500 US. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
